DEFINITION = r"""\begin{definition}[The click]\label{def:click}
A detector set is a subset of Nodes with a declared rung $W$, one set per Node where a screen is read per Node (an input of kind 3, the state and the apparatus). A record's wave runs on the free Nodes and passes through the set's cells; the detector's pointer accumulates its own record's motion across its cells in the units of the conserved form (Section~\ref{sec:massive}), and the click is the comparison
\begin{equation}\label{eq:click}
\boxed{\,W \times (\text{the pointer}) \ge (\text{the record's norm})\,},
\end{equation}
integers both, the crossing of the first rung $1/W$ of the norm in the detector's own count: an action of the law on the state and not a passive read, the record ending at the detector, its content entering the detector's own record, the click stamped with the detector's own count, its own record's cycles across its cells, which advance with or without light, and with the record's birth stamp \cite[3.1, 8.6]{algebra}; a body writes two click lines: its own count's, one per cycle of its record across its cells, and the foreign record's, at the first rung of that record's pointer on its cells, stamped with the count then. One click per record; its completion is the click at one cell, which ends the record everywhere, its offers deleted \cite[section 10]{postulates}\cite[3.1]{algebra}, the host's one non-local step (P6); the pair's gather is that step on a record of two rows, not a second step. The click's weight, the cell's (Bell's $J^2$ of the joint pointer) and not the rung's pointer, is one bilinear form of the record's element, $\mathbf f^{\mathsf T}\mathbf G\mathbf f$ on the rows or the same form on the record's pair of levels (the paragraph ``One group, two representations''), and then one comparison; the rung's pointer is the record's motion squared across the cells (Section~\ref{sec:massive}); light clicks only at a detector set (a declared set of Nodes with its rung $W$); at a massive body's cells light does not click but drives the body's record through the coupling, and the body's own click reads that; a free Node has no rung, no count and no reading. For a pair, one record with two arms and two joint labels $00$ and $11$ of weight $1$ each, $\psi = (00) + (11)$ in $\Z^2 \otimes \Z^2$ tensored with its phase in $\Z[\Z_{\Nphi}]$, entangled by construction, each arm's rows moving on its own line with nothing of one arm written on the other: a detector's setting is the integer $s$ of its window, and at the detector the arm's label pair is rotated by the integer matrix $\mathbf U_s$ of the half-angle tables at $1/256$, $\mathbf U_s^{\mathsf T}\mathbf U_s = n_s\mathbf I$ exactly (Theorem~\ref{th:isometry}), acting on the record's pair of levels by the linear form, one division per output, no phase read and no register \cite[section 14]{declarations}. The click of the pair is one gather at the completion over the four joint cells $(o_A, o_B)$ with the weights
\begin{equation}
R(o_A, o_B) = J(o_A, o_B)^2,\qquad J(o_A, o_B) = \sum_\ell c_\ell\,U_a[o_A][\ell]\,U_b[o_B][\ell],
\label{eq:joint}
\end{equation}
$c_\ell = (1, 1)$ in every world here; the birth wheel's $u \in \Z_{\Nphi}$, the lamp's phase at the birth, selects one cell through the rung on the cumulative weights $C_1 \le \dots \le C_K$ in the declared order,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\,\rung_k = \left\lfloor \frac{2\Nphi C_k + C_K}{2C_K} \right\rfloor \quad (\rung_0 = 0,\ \rung_K = \Nphi),
\qquad \text{the cell is the } k \text{ with } \rung_{k-1} \le u < \rung_k\,},
\label{eq:rung}
\end{equation}
the pair's declaration \cite[3.6]{algebra}, the coarse rung over $o_A$ followed by the fine rung over $o_B$ within it with the same $u$ being how Theorem~\ref{th:marginals} states it; the settings chosen freely of the record and the reading with $c_1 = 1$ (P10) are assumed of the world; no Hilbert space is assumed. The non-locality sits in one object, the pair's record of tensor rank 2, carried by local verbs on two arms and read by one gather at two clicks.
\end{definition}

"""
